## The Inflammatory Response

### Physiology and pathology of inflammation

### Investigations in inflammation

- **Laboratory changes a/w inflammation:**
  - Anaemia of chronic disease (increased hepcidin release requires chronic inflammation)
  - Leukocytosis (reflects transit of activated neutrophils and monocytes to the site of infections)
  - Thrombocytosis (acute phase response)
  - ESR/ CRP
  - Other +ve acute phase reactants include fibrinogen, ferritin, complement components
  - Albumin is a -ve acute phase reactant
- **C-reactive protein** (CRP):
  - <u>Physiology of CRP</u> - role in **opsonisation of pathogens**:
    - Acute phase reactant synthesized and release by the liver under IL-6 influence
    - Up to 1000x rise in CRP after an inflammatory stimulus
    - **Short half life** (~19h), hence a **good marker of acute inflammation**
  - <u>Limitations of CRP</u> - some diseases may not result in rise of CRP despite active inflammation (paradoxically this makes CRP a <u>good marker of infection</u> in these conditions):
    - SLE
    - Systemic sclerosis
    - Ulcerative colitis (but not Crohn's disease)
    - Leukaemia
- **Erythrocyte sedimentation rate:**
  - **Principles of measuring ESR** - an indirect measure of inflammation through measurement of how fast erythrocytes fall through anticoagulated blood:
    - <u>Erythrocytes fall rapidly when they aggregate</u> (i.e. high sedimentation rate):
      - They are normally negatively charged, thus preventing them from clumping such that they sediment slowly
      - When they aggregate (rouleaux formation), there is increased mass/surface area ratio resulting in faster sedimentation
    - <u>Aggregation of erythrocytes is governed by 2 factors</u> - 1) composition of plasma proteins (presence of +ve charged plasma protein promotes clumping through Rouleaux formation), and 2) red cell morphology
  - **Causes of rise in ESR** - most commonly an <u>acute phase response</u>, hence used as a marker of inflammation:
    - Acute-phase plasma proteins (concomittent rise in CRP)
    - Monoclonal or polyclonal rise in Ig (no rise in CRP)
  - **Cause of inappropriately low ESR** - abnormal red cell morphology, which prevents Rouleoux formation:
    - Sickle cell anaemia
    - Spherocytosis irrespective of cause
- **Conditions a/w changes w/ abnormal CRP and/or ESR:** 
![](../img/causes_of_abnormal_esr_crp.png)
- **Plasma viscosity** - not routinely used; same principles as ESR but unaffected by membrane properties of the erythrocyte

### Unexplained raised ESR

### Periodic fever syndromes

- **Definition** - rare disorders characterised by recurrent episodes of fever, systemic inflammation, associated w/ an elevated acute phase response
- **Familial Mediterranean fever:**
  - <u>Epidemiology</u>:
    - Most common familial periodic fever syndrome
    - Mainly affects Mediterranean people, including Arabs, Turks, Sepharic Jews, and Armenians
  - <u>Genetics and pathophysiology of FMF</u>:
    - **Mutation of MEFV gene** - resulting in production of **abnormal pyrin**, an protein that **inhibits production of IL-1 and hence neutrophilic inflammation**
    - **Attacks of neutrophilic inflammation** - fever a/w painful serositis lasting for hours to days
  - <u>Clinical manifestations</u> - 1st attack in 20y, recurrent attacks lasting hours to 4d, while completely asymptomatic in between:
    - Fever
    - Painful serositis (e.g. peritonitis, pleuritis)
    - Arthritis
  - <u>Complications</u> - AA amyloidosis and the nephrotic syndrome
  - <u>Mx</u> - colcicine (effective to reduce number of febrile episodes)
- **Mevalonate kinase deficiency** - previously known as hyper-IgD syndrome (HIDS):
  - <u>Epidemiology</u>:
    - Mainly affects Western Europeans, particularly the Netherlands and northern France
    - Inherited in **autosomal recessive pattern**
  - <u>Pathophysiology</u> - unsure how a mevalonate kinase (lipid metaboism) can result in periodic fevers
  - <u>Clinical features</u> - recurrent attacks of:
    - Fever
    - Abdominal Sx
    - Lymphadenopathy
    - Arthralgias
    - Mucocutaneous lesions
  - <u>Ix</u>:
    - Raised IgG and IgA levels persistently (even in between episodes)
    - CRP raised during acute attacks
  - <u>Mx</u> - no effective Tx
- **TNF receptor-associated periodic syndrome** (TRAPS):
  - <u>Epidemiology</u> - poorly defined, has only been reported in some ethnic groups
  - <u>Genetics</u> - autosomal recessive inheritence of defective TNFRSF1A gene
  - <u>Clinical manifestations</u> - prolonged attacks of:
    - Fever
    - Myalgia
    - Serositis
    - Rash
  - <u>Ix</u>:
    - Neutrophilia
    - Raised CRP
    - Elevated IgA levels
    - Low serum levels of soluble TNF receptors
  - <u>Complications</u> - amyloidosis and the nephrotic syndrome
  - <u>Mx</u> - systemic corticosteroids, TNF receptor therapy, IL-1 antagonists (anakinra)

### Amyloidosis

- **Definition** - heterogenous group of acquired and hereditary disorders characterised by extracellular deposition of insoluble proteins
- **Forms of amyloidosis:** 
![](../img/acquired_and_hereditary_amyloidosis_davidson.png)
  - **Acquired systemic amyloidosis:**
    - <u>Reactive amyloidosis</u> (AA) - increased production of serum amyloid A as a part of a prolonged or recurrent acute inflammatory response (e.g. TB, CTD, FMF), primarily affects the kidneys
    - <u>Light chain amyloidosis</u> (AL) - increased production of monoclonal light chains due to plasma cell dyscrasia and related disorders, known to deposit in the heart, nerves, kidneys, and soft tissue (macroglossia pathognomonic)
    - <u>Dialysis-associated amyloidosis</u> (A-beta-2M) - increased deposition of beta-2 microglobulin due to failure of renal catabolism in kidney failure, known to cause carpal tunnel syndrome and pone deposition
    - <u>Senile systemic amyloidosis</u> - deposition of normal transthyretin protein, usually asymptomatic and increases in incidence w/ age (\>90% of 90y)
  - **Hereditary systemic amyloidosis** - heterogenous group of disorders resulting in abnormal protein production predisposing to amyloid formation, known to affect the nerves and heart, although renal involvement is unusual
- **Pathophysiology of amyloidosis:**
  - \_<u>PProduction of specific proteins</u> - nature of protein depends on underlying cause
  - <u>Extracellular complex deposits</u> - often forming fibrils of the specific protein involved, linked to GAGs, PGs, and serum amyloid P; ultimately resulting in organ dysfunction
- **End-organ damage in amyloid disorders:**
  - <u>Cardiac</u> - restrictive or dilated CM
  - <u>Renal</u> - nephrotic syndrome, tubulointerstitial disease (CIN, RTA)
  - <u>Peripheral nerves</u> - peripheral neuropathy
  - <u>Endocrinopathies</u> - thyroid, adrenal
  - <u>GI</u> - ?xerostomia, motility disorders (gastroparesis, chronic intestinal pseudoobstruction)
- **Clinical suspicion of amyloidosis** - should be considered in all cases of unexplained:
  - 1\. Nephrotic syndrome
  - 2\. Cardiomyopathy
  - 3\. Peripheral neuropathy
- **Dx** - histological Dx based on Bx of an affected organ, rectum, or subcutaneous rfat:
  - Presence of <u>apple-green birefringence</u> of amyloid deposits on <u>Congo red dye</u> under polarised light is pathognomonic
  - Quantitative scintigraphy w/ <u>radio-labelled serum amyloid P</u> to determine over load and distribution
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Supportive care</u> - best medical Tx to support the function of affected organs
    - <u>Tx of underlying cause</u> - in acquired disorders, capable of reducing amyloid deposition aand possibly regression of existing amyloid deposits
    - <u>Tx of hereditary aTTr amyloidosis</u> - LT as the only definitive Tx

