## Allergy

### Pathology of allergy

- **Definition** - allergy is defined as a reaction due to exposure to an otherwise harmless exogenous substance (allergen), <u>mediated by a type 1 hypersensitivity reaction</u>
- **Pathophysiology of allergic response:**
  - **Exposure of allergen to activated B cells** - resulting in prodution of IgE specific to the allergen in the initial exposure phase
  - **Binding of IgE to mast cell** - IgE binds to local mass cells via high-affinity IgE receptors, which is not associated w/ any clinical sequelae
  - **Re-exposure of the same allergen results in mass cell activation** - allergen binds to membrane-bound IgE resulting in mast cell activation and release of variety of mediators:
    - <u>Early-phase response</u> - release of eosinophilic granules consisting of histamine, tryptase, vasoactive peptides and chemotaxic factors that forms the basis of Sx of allergic reaction 
    ![](../img/immediate_hypersensitivity_response.png)
    - <u>Late-phase response</u> - persistent activation of mast cells and substances subsequently synthesized by mast cell results in a late-stage response a/w ongoing swelling and local inflammation (anti-histamines are no-longer effective) 
    ![](../img/products_of_mast_cell_degranulation.png)
- **Susceptibility to allergic disease** - several hypothesis to explain an increase in incidence of allergic disease:
  - <u>Hygiene theory</u> - infections in early life are critically important in maturation of the immune response and bias the immune system against development of allergies
  - <u>Genetic factor</u> - wide array of genetic determinant including genes controlling innate and adaptive immune responses, hence FHx is an important determinent of disease susceptibility
  - <u>Environmental factors</u>:
    - Bacterial or viral infections
    - Pollutants
    - Cigarette smoke
- **Common allergic conditions:** 
![](../img/common_allergic_conditions.png)

### Presenting problems in allergy

1.  General approach to the allergic patient

2.  Anaphylaxis

    - **Definition** - a potentially life-threatening, acute, severe, systemic allergic reaction
    - **Clinical features of anaphylaxis** - acute, immediate onset of Sx from allergen exposure: 
    ![](../img/clinical_features_of_anaphylaxis.png)
      - <u>Skin and mucosal manifestations</u> - e.g. urticaria, conjunctival injection, angioedema of the limps or mucous membrane
      - <u>Respiratory manifestations</u> - respiratory comprimise due to upper airway (e.g. angioedema of the tongue), or laryngeal obstruction (e.g. stridor, wheezing, SOB, hypoxaemia)
      - <u>Cardiovascular manifestations</u> - hypotension and a/w end-organ dysfunction (e.g. syncope, incontinence)
      - <u>Gastrointestinal manifestations</u> - crampy abdominal pain, diarrhoea, repetitive vomiting
    - **Salient points of Hx:**
      - <u>Possible allergens identified in association w/ onset of Sx</u> - onset of Sx within minutes or hours of intake of food, contact w/ latex, insect venoms or drugs: 
      ![](../img/common_allergens_causing_anaphylaxis.png)
      - <u>Potentiating factors</u> - exercise or alcohol can lower the threshold for an anaphylactic event
      - <u>Detailed allergic Hx</u> - detailed Hx of the past 24h is helpful if allergen not immediately obvious
    - **Signs of anaphylaxis:**
      - **Primary assessment** - airway, breathing and circulation, disability and exposure:
        - <u>Airway</u> - angioedema of the limps, tongue, mucous membrane (airway potentially comrpimised)
        - <u>Breathing</u> - check SpO2 for hypoxaemia:
          - Inspiratory stridor suggestive of laryngeal oedema
          - Generalised wheeze suggestive of bronchospasm
        - <u>Circulation</u> - generalised oedematous state, distributive shock, reflex
        - <u>Disability</u> - LOC due to syncope may occur
        - <u>Exposure</u> - generalised urticaria
    - **DDx of anaphylaxis** - anaphylactic mimics:
      - <u>DDx for LOC</u> - cardiac arrhythmias, MI, vasovagal syncope, hypoglycaemia
      - <u>DDx for respiratory distress</u> - status asthmaticus
      - <u>DDx for laryngeal obstruction</u> - foreign body, C1 inhibitor deficiency, idiopathic angioedema
      - <u>DDx of generalised flushing</u> - systemic mastocytosis, carcinoid syndrome
      - <u>Others</u> - e.g. phaeochromocytoma
    - **Anaphylactoid reactions** - non-specific degranuation of mast cells by drugs, chemicals or other triggers and does not involve IgE:
      - <u>Drugs</u> - ASA, NSAID, radiocontrast media, opiates
      - <u>Physical</u> - exercie, cold
      - <u>Idiopathic</u> - no cause identified in up to 20% patients
    - **Ix:**
      - **Serum mass cell tryptase concentration** - useful for retrospective Dx:
        - <u>Timing</u> - taken at 0h and \> 6h
        - <u>Interpretation</u>:
          - Rise of \> 20% from baseline (i.e. \> 6h) is diagnostic
      - **Specific IgE test or skin prick test** - to identify the allergen subsequently
    - **Mx:**
      - **Principles of Mx** - anaphylaxis is an acute medical emergency:
        - <u>General measures</u> - prevent further contact w/ allergen, secure airway, breathing and circulation
        - <u>Prompt intramuscular adrenaline</u> - administer IM adrenaline promptly and repeat if initial response indequate
        - <u>Additional pharmacological therapy</u> - IM or IV antihistamines +/- IV corticosteroids
        - <u>Patient education</u> - education patient and family on how to prevent subsequent episodes, and how to react

        
        ![](../img/emergency_mx_of_anaphylaxis.png)
      - **General measures** - primary assessment and Mx:
        - **Airway** - consider <u>endotracheal intubation</u> if LOC and/or evidence of laryngeal oedema
        - **Breathing:**
          - <u>Give O2</u> to maintain SpO2
          - <u>Nebulised beta-agonists</u> to decrease bronchoconstriction
        - **Circulation** - IV access; IV fluid (20 ml/kg) to restore or maintain BP
      - **IM adrenaline** - delayed administration a/w increased mortality:
        - <u>Dosing</u> - 0.5 mL 1:1000 solution for adults
        - <u>Repeated dosing</u> - repeat at 5-min intervals if initial response inadequate
      - **Additional pharmacological therapy:**
        - <u>IM or IV antihistamine</u> - chlorphenamine
        - <u>IV glucocorticoids</u> - hydrocortisone 200 mg IV
      - **Patient education:**
        - Strict allergen avoidance
        - Anti-histamine for milder reactions
        - Adrenaline autoinjector (AAI) and emergency medical attention

        
        ![](../img/prescription_of_adrenaline_autoinjector.png)
    - **Poor prognostic factors** - increasing risk of death with:
      - Poorly-controlled pre-existing asthma
      - Delayed treatment with adrenaline

3.  Angioedema

    - **Definition** - episodic, localised non-pitting swelling of the submucous or subcutanoeous tissue
    - **Types of angioedema:** 
    ![](../img/types_of_angioedema.png)

### Specific allergies

### C1 inhibitor deficiency

1.  Hereditary angioedema

    - **Definition** - autosomal dominant disorder caused by decreased production or activity of C1 inhibitor protein
    - **Physiological role of C1 inhibitor:**
      - <u>Regulatory protein for the complement cascade</u> - inhibits spontaneous activation of the classical complement cascade
      - <u>Regulatory protein in the kinin cascade</u> - inhibits bradykinin expression
    - **Pathophysiology of HAE** - pathogenic process may be spontaneous or triggered by local trauma or infection:
      - <u>Decreased production or activity of C1 inhibitor</u> - resulting in local bradykinin accumulation
      - <u>Increased local bradykinin levels</u> - results in:
        - Stimulation of pain receptors - local pain
        - Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability - local swelling
    - **Common sites affected by HAE:**
      - Face
      - Extremities
      - Upper airways (laryngeal oedema is often triggered by minor dental procedures and are often fatal)
      - GI tract
    - **Clinical features of HAE** - first episode typically in adolescence, but may go undiagnosed for years:
      - **Angioedema** - usually self-limiting, not a/w wheals, precipitated by local tissue damage or spontaneously, takes around 48h to resolve
      - **Additional clinical features** - dependent of site of infection:
        - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - oedema of the intestinal wall may result in obstruction or severe ischaemia
        - <u>Facial swelling</u> - angioedema of the faces, particularly the lips and tongue
    - **Ix:**
      - **C4** - characteristically low in acute episodes
      - **C1 inhibitor measurement** - low levels is diagnositic of HAE
    - **Mx:**
      - Purified C1 inhibitor or bradykinin receptor antagonist (icatibant) for severe attacks
      - Danazol (modified androgens) for disease remission

2.  Acquired C1 inhibitor deficiency

    - **Definition** - acquired decreased production or activity of C1 inhibitor protein
    - **Etiology** - a/w immune dysregulation:
      - Autoimmune disorders
      - Lymphoproliferative disorders

